# HomeProxy Development Notes

This file tracks the development direction for luci-app-homeproxy. HomeProxy is
an ImmortalWrt LuCI application around sing-box, UCI, fw4/nftables, dnsmasq, and
service init scripts, so changes should be evaluated as part of that whole
router workflow.

## Project Direction

- Keep HomeProxy predictable on ImmortalWrt first. OpenWrt behavior is useful
  reference material, but runtime checks and package testing should use an
  ImmortalWrt target whenever possible.
- Treat UI, UCI config, subscription import, sing-box generation, firewall
  rules, DNS handling, and service startup as one connected flow.
- Prefer gentle fixes that preserve user configuration, fail open when startup
  setup cannot be completed, and avoid leaving traffic in a half-captured state.
- Keep upstream PRs small and focused. Use the personal branch for integrated
  builds where multiple fixes need to be tested together.

## Current Improvement State

These changes are integrated in the personal development branch:

- ACL domain rules are applied reliably after saving ACL-only changes.
- Direct-domain rules are honored in TUN mode by feeding dnsmasq nftsets.
- Startup setup failures fail open instead of leaving traffic half-captured.
- Legacy Shadowsocks share links are accepted during subscription import.
- Stale main node references are cleared when subscription nodes are removed.
- Invalid VLESS share links are rejected instead of imported as broken nodes.
- Subscription URLs are normalized before download, so raw non-ASCII query
  values do not break wget/uclient-fetch.
- A dedicated LuCI log view is available for HomeProxy logs.
- Built-in rule set bootstrap avoids using the proxy path before it exists.
- Subscription node page rendering is improved for large subscription sets.

## Open TODO

- [x] Improve subscription page performance
  The subscription node page has been optimized for large imported node sets.
  Keep future UI changes focused on preserving this lower render cost.

- [ ] Refactor nft rules
  Simplify and document nftables rule generation. The goal is to make routing,
  ACL, TUN, DNS redirect, and dnsmasq nftset behavior easier to reason about.
  Direct-domain nftset handling has been improved, but the broader nft rule
  refactor is still open.

- [ ] Move ACL settings to a dedicated page
  ACL settings currently share space with other client settings. Moving them to
  their own page should make rule configuration clearer and easier to maintain.

- [ ] Other improvements
  Keep small fixes and cleanup focused, tested, and easy to review.

## Development Principles

- Prefer one fix per branch and one clean scoped commit per upstream PR.
  Use branch names such as `fix/subscription-vless-validation` or
  `fix/subscription-url-normalization`.
- Use Conventional Commit titles, for example
  `fix(subscribe): reject invalid VLESS links`.
- Keep upstream-targeted fixes based on the latest `origin/dev`.
- Use the personal branch only for integrated builds and device testing.
- Follow the existing code style:
  - keep SPDX headers and `'use strict';` where the file already has them;
  - use tabs for indentation in JavaScript and ucode files;
  - keep ucode helpers small and local unless they are shared by multiple
    scripts;
  - prefer UCI, ubus, dnsmasq, fw4, and sing-box helpers already used by the
    project instead of ad-hoc shell parsing;
  - do not change LuCI wording or translations unless the UI behavior changes.
- Do not silently accept invalid subscription nodes. Parser behavior should
  match the LuCI import path and reject missing required fields before writing
  UCI sections.
- Do not rewrite user-entered subscription URLs in the UI just to make downloads
  work. Normalize URLs at the download boundary before calling wget.

## Validation Checklist

Run the smallest checks that cover the touched area:

- JavaScript syntax:
  `node --check htdocs/luci-static/resources/view/homeproxy/node.js`
- Whitespace and patch hygiene:
  `git diff --check`
- Subscription parser changes:
  run `/etc/homeproxy/scripts/update_subscriptions.uc` on an ImmortalWrt test
  device and inspect `/var/run/homeproxy/homeproxy.log`.
- Client generator changes:
  run the generator and `sing-box check` on the generated client config.
- Package changes:
  build the APK/IPK through GitHub Actions and install the matching artifact on
  the target ImmortalWrt device.

## Development References

Use these references to understand the platform expectations before making a
change:

- ImmortalWrt source tree:
  https://github.com/immortalwrt/immortalwrt
- OpenWrt package development:
  https://openwrt.org/docs/guide-developer/packages
- OpenWrt APK package manager:
  https://openwrt.org/docs/guide-user/additional-software/apk
- LuCI JavaScript APIs:
  https://openwrt.github.io/luci/jsapi/
- ucode language and modules:
  https://ucode.mein.io/
- OpenWrt firewall configuration:
  https://openwrt.org/docs/guide-user/firewall/firewall_configuration
- dnsmasq manual:
  https://thekelleys.org.uk/dnsmasq/docs/dnsmasq-man.html
- sing-box configuration:
  https://sing-box.sagernet.org/configuration/

Local orientation:

- LuCI views live under `htdocs/luci-static/resources/view/homeproxy/`.
- Runtime ucode scripts live under `root/etc/homeproxy/scripts/`.
- Service lifecycle is handled by `root/etc/init.d/homeproxy`.
- Menu, translation, CI, and package metadata live under `root/usr/share/`,
  `po/`, `.github/`, and the package `Makefile`.
